html.append('''<h2 id="synthesis">Final synthesis</h2><h3>Stable observations</h3><p>Both constructs have reproducibly enriched peak subsets. In the original all-nonzero and QN peak comparisons, the UTR-minus-intron preference difference stays negative and small. Under equal-site landmark weighting, 328 relative enrichment is stronger near exonic splice boundaries across the saved scale/pseudocount sensitivities.</p><h3>Processing-sensitive observations</h3><p>The overall magnitude is not stable. Outcome selection, measurable-site masks, pseudocount, selected-library denominator and log/replicate aggregation order all matter. Pooling reads can reverse the direction. Round-two coverage differences weaken substantially after peak selection.</p><h3>Strongest spatial observations</h3><p>Original common-CPM site-weighted donor and acceptor minima are on the exonic side. CLIP-only separation is visible there before SMI normalization. Transcript-end differences are broad and involve both components. Round-two genome-wide scaled profiles show different intron and 3′UTR coverage tendencies, which must be described as depth patterns.</p><h3>Major technical limitations</h3><p>Two biological pairs per construct, sparse CLIP, unequal library sizes, extensive zeros, pseudocount dependence, overlapping gene/feature models, selection effects, and a single sampling seed limit inference. Gene/site uncertainty does not replace biological-replicate uncertainty. Peak significance in one construct alone does not prove an interaction, and these analyses do not establish altered splicing.</p><h3>Open analyses</h3><p>The direct construct × assay count interaction is proposed, not run. Source extraction workflows and the historical joint-positive generator are not fully reconstructable locally. Site-distribution and gene-stratified proximity diagnostics are completed; undefined sparse-stratum intervals are explicitly retained.</p><h3>Immediate grant-use options</h3><p>Use the donor or acceptor component panels to communicate the localization hypothesis while making the SMI contribution visible. Pair them with weighting sensitivity. Use the original/QN continuous pair to communicate the modest region effect honestly. Include a support-distribution or peak-proximity companion when describing how broadly the spatial observation applies. Choose based on the claim to communicate, not the largest visual separation.</p></main><script>document.getElementById('search').addEventListener('input',function(){let q=this.value.toLowerCase();document.querySelectorAll('#registry tbody tr').forEach(r=>r.hidden=!r.textContent.toLowerCase().includes(q))});document.querySelectorAll('a[href^="#"]').forEach(a=>a.addEventListener('click',()=>{let d=document.getElementById(a.hash.slice(1));if(d&&d.tagName==='DETAILS')d.open=true}));</script></body></html>''')
